{
  "subtype": "what_if",
  "outcome_variable": "resolution_duration",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "incident_category",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "Categorizes the operational burden type described in text; the query targets eliminating 'operational burdens', so this is the treatment lever, though category itself is more a descriptor than a settable action."
    },
    {
      "name": "failure_type",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "Represents the kind of failure/burden described; aligns with the 'operational burdens' to be eliminated. Borderline intervenable — it is a diagnosed condition rather than a lever an actor sets."
    },
    {
      "name": "affected_system_scope",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "System scope (server_side, infrastructure) plausibly influences both the type of burden and the resolution time; a common cause not named in the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "reassignment_indicator",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Reassignment typically occurs on the path between the burden/incident and the duration outcome, so it is a mediator, not a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurrence_signal",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Recurrence could drive both the nature of the burden and duration; near-degenerate (496/4) so weak, but conceptually a common cause not named."
    },
    {
      "name": "scope_indicator",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether an issue is individual vs system_wide can affect both burden type and duration; a plausible common cause not named."
    },
    {
      "name": "time_of_day_band",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Time incident occurs (after_hours/overnight) affects staffing/response and correlates with incident type; a pre-treatment common cause not named in query."
    },
    {
      "name": "assigned_agent",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Agent assignment could be argued as mediator, but agent skill/experience is a common cause of both handling and duration; treated as a confounder not named. Assignment is itself intervenable."
    },
    {
      "name": "caller_segment",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Caller identity/segment can influence both the kind of issue reported and resolution time; a common cause not named in the query."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["incident_category", "failure_type"],
  "treatment_intervenable": false,
  "confounder_quality": 0.45,
  "rationale": "The query asks an interventional what-if: eliminate the most common operational burdens and estimate the drop in resolution duration. The added columns identify the treatment via incident_category/failure_type, but these encode a diagnosed condition (an intrinsic classification of what went wrong) rather than a lever an actor can SET, CHANGE, or DECIDE — you cannot straightforwardly 'set' a connectivity_failure to none as an operational intervention. Thus the intervenability requirement (a) is not clearly satisfied. On confounders (b), the set does include several plausible pre-treatment common causes NOT named in the query — affected_system_scope, scope_indicator, time_of_day_band, caller_segment — which is the capability under test and earns partial credit. However quality is diluted by a mediator misclassified as a covariate (reassignment_indicator), a near-degenerate signal (recurrence_signal, 496/4), and assigned_agent which sits ambiguously between mediator and confounder. Overall: treatment present but weakly intervenable; moderate, mixed-quality confounder coverage."
}
```

